INT. BAKERY TASTING ROOM - DAY

White tablecloths, little forks, six slices of cake on six small plates. A chandelier made of whisks.

GABRIEL ROSSETTI-OKEKE, thirties, sits very straight beside his fiance THEO ANDRZEJEWSKI, thirties, who is already eating. Across from them, PASTRY CHEF MARGUERITE DUVALIER, fifties, stands with her hands folded, wearing the placid smile of someone who has seen every possible argument over frosting. He lifts his fork, takes a bite of the lemon, closes his eyes, and sets the fork down with great care, and he turns to the other man, and he whispers something that makes him laugh with his mouth full, and he waves a hand, spraying crumbs, and then he points the fork at the chef.

THEO
This is the one.

GABRIEL
We haven't tried the others.

THEO
We don't need to.

MARGUERITE
Take your time. Some couples spend hours.

GABRIEL
We're not some couples.

THEO
We have a flight at four.

MARGUERITE
Of course.

He pushes the plate aside, reaches for the vanilla, tastes it, and nods, and the other one nods back, and they look at each other, and each of them lifts a fork and feeds the other a bite of the third slice, solemn as a communion.

GABRIEL
Oh.

THEO
Oh.

MARGUERITE
(beaming)
That's the almond.

GABRIEL
We'll take the almond.

THEO
And the lemon on the top tier.

MARGUERITE
Lovely. I'll draw up the order.

She disappears into the kitchen. The room is quiet. A clock ticks. Theo slides his hand across the tablecloth and takes Gabriel's.

THEO (CONT'D)
Can I tell you something?

GABRIEL
Always.

THEO
I didn't want the cake.

GABRIEL
What?

THEO
I wanted the excuse to sit here and watch you pick.

Gabriel stares at him. Then, with enormous dignity, he picks up the lemon slice and presses it into his own face.

The kitchen door swings open. Marguerite stands there with a clipboard and takes it in without blinking.

MARGUERITE
Shall I note "fruit-forward"?

GABRIEL
(through lemon)
Please.
